ef 1079 #uuid "931b45ae-bf74-5d0e-9144-dd8d9e9c7b50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186570447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570656 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a5ac821f-77cc-50ee-b292-5973c2a64068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186570574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570681 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "db33a278-5e86-591a-8a62-14279c3317f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570699 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(B)=-21.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{4}$ (switching the first and the fourth rows). What is the value of $det(C)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f13da88-9214-5888-9868-a4b7f8edacdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570699 #edb/ref 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-det(B) \\\\  & =-(-21) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570735 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570735 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570735 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(B)=-5.$ What is the value of $det(3B)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570735 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "620b0d62-bee7-51dc-8bf7-49326cfad693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570735 #edb/ref 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot B) &={k}^{n}\\cdot det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=3$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(3B) & ={3}^{2}\\cdot det(B) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570738 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570738 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570738 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=80.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{4}:=\\frac{3}{10}{R}_{4}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570738 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "687e18db-1843-51e7-a8a0-475f4a6d1676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570738 #edb/ref 1074 "The row operation applied to $B$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{3}{10}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{3}{10}det(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot 80 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570763 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570763 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570763 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=11.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{4}:=3{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1f0bc2a-d097-538a-b002-360896d3c91f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570763 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}:=3{R}_{4}$ involves multiplying a row by $3,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $3$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $3.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3det(A) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570776 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570776 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570776 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570776 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=3$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570776 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7915d65-a649-5e77-8661-8aa533c4bd16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570776 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $1$st and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(3) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570789 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570789 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570789 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(C)=-5.$ What is the value of $det(-4C)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570789 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9fbd8ca-7a83-50db-a6d3-a36d5d6ceb97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570789 #edb/ref 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot C) &={k}^{n}\\cdot det(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(-4C) & =(-4)^{3}\\cdot det(C) \\\\  & =(-64)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =320.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
